## Founding & History
Tolpuddle Vineyard was established in 1988 as a joint venture by Dr. Tony Jordan, Garry Crittenden, and the Casimaty family. The vineyard is named after the Tolpuddle Martyrs, English farm laborers who were transported to Australia in the 1830s for attempting to form an agricultural union. George Loveless, a leader of the Martyrs, served part of his sentence on a property near Richmond, a portion of which is now Tolpuddle Vineyard.

Initially, the fruit from Tolpuddle Vineyard was sold to various businesses, including Penfolds for their Yattarna Chardonnay, Chandon for sparkling base, and Hardy's for their Eileen Hardy Chardonnay. In 2006, Tolpuddle Vineyard won the inaugural Tasmanian Vineyard of the Year award.

In 2011, Michael Hill Smith MW and Martin Shaw, cousins and co-proprietors of Shaw + Smith Wines in South Australia, purchased the vineyard. They had visited Tasmania with no initial intention of buying a vineyard but were "smitten" by Tolpuddle. The debut vintage of Tolpuddle Chardonnay and Pinot Noir under their ownership was in 2012.

## People
The current owners of Tolpuddle Vineyard are Michael Hill Smith MW and Martin Shaw. Michael Hill Smith is Australia's first Master of Wine.

Adam Wadewitz is the chief winemaker. He joined Tolpuddle in 2013 for his first vintage. Vineyard manager is Carlos Souris, who has over 30 years of grapegrowing experience in Tasmania. Georgie Jacobs also joined as vineyard manager in 2025. David LeMire handles sales and marketing. Pip Anderson, a hospitality veteran from Domaine A, was recruited to run the cellar door.

## Vineyard & Site
Tolpuddle Vineyard spans approximately 30 to 31 hectares, fully planted. Some sources state 20 hectares (50 acres) were originally planted. The vineyard is dedicated exclusively to Chardonnay and Pinot Noir. It comprises 12 blocks of Chardonnay and 14 blocks of Pinot Noir, with 13 hectares being new plantings not yet in production as of October 2022. The plantings are roughly 50% Chardonnay and 50% Pinot Noir.

The vineyard is situated on a long, even, northeast-facing slope in the Coal River Valley. The soil type is light silica sand over fractured sandstone, which is free-draining and nutrient-poor, forcing the vines to struggle. This soil type is considered ideal for curtailing plant vigor and driving intensity in the grapes. The climate is one of Australia's coolest and driest, with around 500mm of annual rainfall, which helps reduce disease pressure. Cool nights extend the growing season and preserve acidity.

The farming approach focuses on soil regeneration, composting, and cover cropping. Herbicides have been phased out. The newer sections of the vineyard are planted at nearly double the density of the original blocks to focus on quality over quantity. While the estate is not organically certified, most of the vineyard is farmed organically, and they transitioned to organic viticulture in 2021. Water conservation is prioritized through targeted irrigation and soil aeration.

## Cellar Door Experience
Tolpuddle Vineyard recently opened a tasting room, which operates quietly and by appointment only. Bookings are essential and require full payment at the time of reservation. Reservations open three months in advance and future dates are added monthly.

The tasting room is an intimate space overlooking the vines. Guests can enjoy a guided tasting of current-release Chardonnay and Pinot Noir, along with a carefully selected back-vintage example. The experience typically lasts 40-60 minutes. The cellar door is open Friday to Monday from 11 AM to 5 PM.

## Food & Hospitality
A seasonal Larder menu is available to complement the wine tasting. The Tasting Room can be hired for private functions and events for up to 32 guests, with outside catering options available.

## Awards & Recognition
Tolpuddle Vineyard has received significant awards and recognition:
* **2006:** Won the inaugural Tasmanian Vineyard of the Year award.
* **2018 Vintage Chardonnay:** Named World's Best White Wine at the International Wine Challenge (IWC).
* **2020 Vintage Chardonnay:** Named Best Australian White Wine at the IWC.
* **2021 Vintage Chardonnay:** Named White Wine of the Year by Decanter magazine, receiving a perfect score of 100 points.
* **2021 Vintage Pinot Noir:** Named Best Australian Red Wine and won the Australian Pinot Noir Trophy at the IWC.
* **2023 Vintage Chardonnay:** Named Champion White Wine at the 2025 International Wine Challenge in London, scoring 98 points. It also collected the International Chardonnay Trophy, Best Australian White, and Best Tasmanian Chardonnay. James Suckling named the newly released 2024 vintage Australian Wine of the Year.
* **2023 Vintage Chardonnay:** Swept the top Chardonnay trophies at the Melbourne Royal Wine Awards. Dave Brookes scored it 98 points.
* **2023 Vintage Pinot Noir:** Secured the Australian Pinot Noir Trophy. Dave Brookes scored it 96 points.
* **2024 Vintage Chardonnay:** Won the François de Castella Trophy for Best Young White Wine at the Melbourne Royal Wine Awards in 2025.

Adam Wadewitz was named "Winemaker of the Year" by Decanter magazine in 2023. Tolpuddle's Chardonnay has won the Françoise De Castella Trophy for Best Young White Wine at the Melbourne Royal Wine Awards three times for the 2018, 2021, and 2023 vintages.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Tolpuddle Vineyard employs sustainable winemaking practices, including meticulous viticulture and low-intervention methods. Herbicides have been phased out, and compost and living cover crops are used to keep soils biologically active and retain moisture. The vineyard transitioned to organic viticulture in 2021, reducing chemical inputs and improving vine resilience. Most of the vineyard is farmed organically. Water conservation is a priority, with targeted irrigation and soil aeration. The estate is not organically certified.

## Visiting Practical Info
Address: 37 Back Tea Tree Road, Richmond, Tasmania, 7025.
Phone: (03) 6145 8800. (Note: Other sources list 08 8155 6003 or (08) 8155 6003.)
Website: http://www.tolpuddlevineyard.com/.
Email: tastingroom@tolpuddlevineyard.com or info@tolpuddlevineyard.com.
Social Media: No reliable information found from public sources.
Distance from major town: Located just outside the historic village of Richmond, approximately a 20-minute drive (26km) from Hobart.

## Notable / Distinctive
Tolpuddle Vineyard is distinctive for its "stubborn commitment to doing things slowly and properly," refusing to expand or compromise on quality, even when it might be more profitable. Despite its international success, the vineyard has no plans to expand beyond its 30-31 hectares, believing that its specific patch of sand and sandstone soil is what makes its wines distinctive. This restraint is considered almost radical in an industry that often equates growth with progress.

The vineyard's unique terroir, characterized by light silica sand over fractured sandstone, forces the vines to struggle, which is seen as the foundation of quality and intensity in their wines. The cool, dry climate of the Coal River Valley also contributes to the wines' tension, purity, and balance.

Tolpuddle's approach to its cellar door is also distinctive; they delayed opening a proper tasting room for years until they felt the visitor experience matched the quality of their wines. The wines are released annually via a waitlist, and demand often outstrips supply, leading to an allocation system.

